\subsection{Reduced share Exposure}

The ordering of \cref{tab:sync-split} alone cannot explain the results, because $16\times8$ has the lowest exposure at every point of \Cref{fig:2d-sync} and \Cref{fig:3d-sync}，in 2D, the worst time
per iteration at all 11 of them. What does track performance is the
size of reduction, comparing its exposure against flat MPI at the same size-node point with its time
per iteration relative to flat MPI can got the \Cref{tab:sync-breakeven}


Write $S_\ell(p)$ for the halo-plus-reduction share of layout $\ell$ at size--node
point $p$, and $t_\ell(p)$ for its time per KSP iteration. The first two columns of
\Cref{tab:sync-breakeven} are the range,

\[
  \mathrm{ExposureRemoved} = S_{128\times1} - S_\ell, \qquad
  \mathrm{TimeRatio} = \frac{t_\ell}{t_{128\times1}},
\]

so $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ $ > 0$ means the layout is less than flat MPI, and $\mathrm{TimeRatio} < 1$
means that layout is faster than flat MPI. For example, $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ is 
$(34.3+13.9)-(16.4+9.9)=21.9$
points for 2D $16\times8$ at 165m on 32 nodes at \Cref{tab:sync-split}.

Break-even, a measure defined here, is computed by $a$ and $b$:
\[
  \mathrm{BreakEven} = \frac{1-b}{a} = \frac{b-1}{|a|}, \qquad a<0,
\]
with none when $a\ge0$, come from a least-squares that fit over each layout pairs:

\[
  \mathrm{TimeRatio} = a\,\mathrm{ExposureRemoved} + b
\]

Here $b$ is the intercept,the time ratio the line gives at $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}=0$:
what a layout would cost against flat MPI if it removed no exposure at all, so $b-1$ is the cost of threading alone, The slope $a$ is the change in $\mathrm{TimeRatio}$ per point of
exposure removed, so $|a|$ is how much of that cost one point pays back, 

Since $a<0$, the fitted line drops below one exactly where $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ passes
$\mathrm{BreakEven}$. The column is therefore a threshold: it is how much exposure a layout
has to remove before threading pays for itself, and the lower it is, the sooner that
happens. The fit is descriptive, so single points can fall on the wrong side of it.

The $r$ column is the Pearson correlation, computed from the same pairs,
\[
  r = \frac{\operatorname{cov}(\mathrm{ExposureRemoved},\ \mathrm{TimeRatio})}
           {\sigma_{\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}}\ \sigma_{\mathrm{TimeRatio}}} \in [-1,1],
\]

their covariance over the product of their standard deviations. It shares the sign of $a$
and says how closely they follow the line, so it measures how firm a break-even is and not pay a
influence to it.

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Reduction in synchronisation exposure against time per iteration, both relative to flat MPI at the same size–node point. The break-even value is the exposure reduction the fitted line requires to offset threading overhead. $a$ and $b$ are that line's slope and intercept, so $(1-b)/a$ reproduces the last column.Eleven points per layout; the fits are descriptive only.}
  \label{tab:sync-breakeven}
  \begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}
    \toprule
    Layout & Exposure removed & Time ratio & $r$ & $10^3a$ & $b$ & Break-even \\
    \midrule
    \multicolumn{7}{@{}l}{\emph{2D}}\\
    $64\times2$ &  0.8--7.4  & 0.872--0.995 & $-0.67$ & $-15.29$ & 1.0178 & 1.2 \\
    $32\times4$ &  5.3--13.7 & 0.937--1.096 & $-0.59$ & $-11.13$ & 1.1305 & 11.7 \\
    $16\times8$ &  6.3--21.9 & 1.294--1.674 & $+0.00$ & $+0.08$  & 1.4313 & none \\
    \addlinespace
    \multicolumn{7}{@{}l}{\emph{3D}}\\
    $64\times2$ &  1.9--15.1 & 0.768--1.022 & $-0.86$ & $-21.60$ & 1.0618 & 2.9 \\
    $32\times4$ &  5.5--23.5 & 0.751--1.070 & $-0.90$ & $-17.47$ & 1.1270 & 7.3 \\
    $16\times8$ & 17.4--31.1 & 0.899--1.122 & $-0.83$ & $-15.82$ & 1.4226 & 26.7 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

Five of the six correlations $r$ are negative and three exceed 0.8: the more exposure a layout removes at a given point, the fast it is to flat MPI here. Read down $b$, the cost of threading before any exposure is removed: it rises sharply at each doubling, and at four and eight threads it is almost independent of the dimension,
1.1305 against 1.1270 and 1.4313 against 1.4226, although flat MPI's exposure share differs
between 2D and 3D by 36 points at the largest size--node point. Only $64\times2$, whose
threading cost is small in both, differs appreciably. Read down $|a|$, the return on one
point of removed exposure: it falls at every step, and at all three layouts it is larger in
3D than in 2D. The break-even column quantifies the trade-off, Each doubling of threads per rank removes more exposure but need more before it pays: 

\Cref{fig:3d-tradeoff} and \Cref{fig:2d-tradeoff} plot the fits, The 3D case is taken first because all three of its layouts behave the same way while the exception the argument turns on lies in 2D. In 3D the three layouts form three rougly parallel descending lines, displaced from each other along the horizontal axis: each removes more exposure than the one before it, and each needs more of it before crossing parity; the bands flatten as
threads per rank rise, which is the $a$ column.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/ksp3d_exposure_tradeoff_by_chain.pdf}
  \caption{3D $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ relative to flat MPI against the resulting time per
  iteration, one point per size--node pair. The solid line is the
  pooled fit over all 11 points, the one \Cref{tab:sync-breakeven} tabulates; the two broken
  lines split those points into their weak-scaling chains, dashed with filled markers for
  $\sim$40k unknowns/core and dash-dotted with hollow markers for $\sim$80k. Where a layout's fitted line crosses parity is the break-even column of \Cref{tab:sync-breakeven}; the lines are descriptive, not
  predictive.}
  \label{fig:3d-tradeoff}
\end{figure}


% to Find the figures:

This explain the two things:

First, why the 3D advantage rises and falls with node count:

Holding a layout and a chain fixed and walking the
node count, the $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ and the $\mathrm{TimeRatio}$ reverse together at every step.
Along the $\sim$40k chain in 3D, $32\times4$ has an $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ of 10.4, 16.8, 13.9, 8.8, 5.5 and 15.3
points from one node to 32, and a $\mathrm{TimeRatio}$ of 0.893, 0.781, 0.844, 0.958, 1.070
and 0.837: five transitions, five reversals, including the recovery at 32 nodes where a
9.8-point rise in $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ turns a 7.0\% loss into a 16.3\% gain. All 18
transitions of $64\times2$ and $32\times4$ across both 3D chains behave this way. The
advantage is non-monotone because $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ is, not because the mechanism
differs.



\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/ksp2d_exposure_tradeoff_by_chain.pdf}
  \caption{2D $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ relative to flat MPI against the resulting time per
  iteration, one point per size--node pair.The solid line is the
  pooled fit over all 11 points, the one \Cref{tab:sync-breakeven} tabulates; the two broken
  lines split those points into their weak-scaling chains, dashed with filled markers for
  $\sim$40k unknowns/core and dash-dotted with hollow markers for $\sim$80k. the lines are descriptive, not
  predictive.}
  \label{fig:2d-tradeoff}
\end{figure}

The second is the threshold itself. 

In 2D, $32\times4$ brackets its own 11.7-point break-even:

Along the $\sim$40k chain: an $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ of 9.6 points at 41m on
eight nodes for a $\mathrm{TimeRatio}$ of 1.003, then 13.7 points at 82m on 16 nodes for
0.944, crossing parity where the fit puts it. Along the $\sim$80k chain its
$\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ never exceeds 8.6 points and it never crosses, bottoming at
1.004. The threshold holds in both directions.

But because the threshold is descriptive, it has a counter-example, and \Cref{fig:2d-tradeoff}
shows what it consists of: its pooled $a$ is positive, so the solid line lies flat and the table returns
no break-even at all (it is pointless), while the two broken lines from one per chain both descend steeply
across it. The flatness is therefore an artefact of pooling. Taken separate the chains correlate at $-0.91$
over the six $\sim$40k points and $-0.96$ over the five $\sim80k$ points, among the tightest fits in these
experiments. Accourding to that, what the layout lacks therefore not a relationship between $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ and speed but the value of the $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$, each chain's line reaches parity only beyond the largest $\mathrm{ExposureRemoved}$ the layout ever attains, 21.9 points, so neither threshold can be stated without extrapolating and neither is ever reached. That it is slower than flat MPI by 29--67\% at every one of its 11 points is a direct observation and needs no fit. Some other cost dominates. 

\Cref{tab:map-shares} identifies taht cost:
at the same problem size and node counts, $16\times8$ spends more of its core-time waiting in the openMP runtime than any other layout, 76.5\% against 48.3\% for $64\times2$ at eight nodes. The two half of the trade are therefor both measured, one on the full matrix without a profiler and the other on eleven profiled cells with one.
What remains unmeasured is their sum: the profiled and unprofiled quantites come from different instruments on
different instruments on different runs and cannot be added into a single budget.


\subsection{What This Does and Does Not Establish}
\label{sec:sync-limits}
Pooling all 33 hybrid measurements of a dimension breaks the relationship, and breaks it differently in each.
In 2D the pooled correlation is $+0.58$, where the two layouts that have a break-even give $-0.67$ and $-0.59$.
In 3D it falls to $+0.10$ from three values that all exceed $0.8$ in magnitude.

The obvious reason is that $16\times8$ removes the most exposure and is also the slowest.
That is enough in 3D: drop it and the pooled figure returns to $-0.75$. It is not enough in
2D, where dropping it still leaves $+0.13$. There, $32\times4$ removes more exposure than
$64\times2$ at all 11 points and is slower at all 11. The ordering between layouts runs
against the ordering within them in both dimensions, and $16\times8$ is only the clearest
case. The relationship holds within a layout, so a pooled figure would mislead.

The two weak-scaling chains cannot be pooled either, and that is a second and
independent effect: in 3D it does little harm, every layout's per-chain break-evens
falling within 2.4 points of its pooled one, while in 2D none of the three layouts
survives unchanged and $16\times8$ inverts the sign. \Cref{tab:sync-breakeven} still
reports pooled fits, because five and six points are too few to fit a chain on its
own, and its columns should be read with that in mind. The $b$ column agrees across
dimensions only because the fits are pooled: split the chains and the agreement
loosens at $32\times4$ and fails on the $\sim$40k chain at $16\times8$. Neither
pooling changes which layouts beat flat MPI, because that is read off the
measurements and not off a fit.

These data show where the layout advantage comes from within PETSc's own event accounting: less exposure to halo exchange; global reduction is second and responds far less to the layout. Together with \Cref{sec:map-matrix} they place the opposing cost in the OpenMP runtime rather than at a memory boundary. Neither shows a hardware
mechanism. Several causes fit each half of the trade equally well, and neither instrument
can tell them apart. \Cref{sec:rq4} names the candidates; \Cref{sec:futurework} gives the
hardware-counter work that would separate them.



\chapter{Discussion}

The four questions of \Cref{sec:aims} are answered in turn. RQ1 and RQ2 rest on \Cref{ch:multinode}, 
RQ3 on \Cref{ch:profiling}, and RQ4 on the limits of both.

\section{RQ1: Does the threaded backend reduce time to solution, and by how much?}
\label{sec:rq1}
Hybrid execution cuts solve time in both dimensions. The two cases answer the
question differently, and what differs is the shape of the result, not which layout wins

In 2D the answer is ordered. Along both unknowns/core chains, every hybrid layout gains on
flat MPI as the node count grows, and four of the six series gain at every step.
$64\times2$ is faster per iteration than flat MPI at all 11 size--node points, and its
margin widens from 4.3\% at one node to 12.8\% at 16. $32\times4$ crosses from 9.6\% slower
at one node to 6.3\% faster at 32. A layout comparison made at small node counts therefore
does not carry over to large ones, which is the practical content of the 2D result.

In 3D the effect is larger but unordered. The best hybrid gain in throughput is 33.2\%,
against 14.7\% in 2D, and two-thirds of the hybrid points are faster per iteration than
flat MPI. No series gains at every step: the advantage peaks at two to four nodes, is gone
by eight to 16, and returns at the largest point. On raw throughput $32\times4$ wins six of
the 11 points, $64\times2$ four, and flat MPI one. The best balance depends on problem size
and node count in a way the 2D data do not show.






\section{RQ2: DAt which balance, and how does the answer change with the regime?}
\label{sec:rq2}
If it does, at which rank--thread balances, and how does the answer change
with problem size, node count, dimensionality, and scaling regime?

